  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nQuiet conditions with above average temperatures are expected\nthrough the remainder of the week and into the weekend with high\npressure over the region. The clear skies and light winds at\nnight will make for efficient radiational cooling, with low\ntemperatures dipping into the low 30s and upper 20s this morning.\nThese large diurnal temperature shifts will continue with the \nhigh pressure in place the next few days, however, as the high \nshifts into the Atlantic over the next couple of days, weak \nsoutherly return flow should help keep the overnight lows a few \ndegrees higher starting tonight. Afternoon highs through Sunday in\nthe mid 50s to low 60s will be 10 to 15 degrees above normal. \nFinally, a very dry airmass will be overhead, but with the cool \novernight temperatures and warm river waters, expect at least \nriver valley fog to still develop the next couple of nights. \n\nThe next chance for rain isn't expected until late Sunday, but \nthere is still a lot of uncertainty with the synoptic setup this\nfar out. A cold front will cross the region, which all guidance\nagrees on, but uncertainty still remains with the track of a \nsfc low along or just off the East Coast. Latest guidance is\ntrending a bit farther east for the sfc low track, but also\nstronger as well. The eastward shift looks like it is due to a\nslower track north along the coast, allowing the cold front to\nadvance a bit farther east, steering it off the coast more. If \nthe sfc low is farther off the coast, then there will be less \nrain with just the potential for showers with the passing cold \nfront. There is still plenty of time for the forecast guidance\nto continue to adjust, both west and east. But most of the\nguidance is trending drier for all of Sunday.",
